<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles X Gustav</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles X Gustav was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the mid-seventeenth century and a gifted military commander. After a daring winter crossing of the frozen straits between Danish islands, he forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> in 1658. The treaty gave Sweden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a> and Norwegian provinces, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> to its greatest extent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav.jpg" alt="Charles X Gustav" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sébastien Bourdon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Charles_X_Gustavus_(S%C3%A9bastien_Bourdon)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_19702.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
